---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import UlidGenerator from '../../components/UlidGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="ULID Generator" description="Generate ULIDs — sortable, timestamp-prefixed unique identifiers, an alternative to UUID.">
  <UlidGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why sortability is the key advantage over UUID</h2>
  <p>A standard UUID v4 is fully random, meaning IDs generated seconds apart sort in no meaningful order — a ULID encodes a timestamp as its prefix, so IDs naturally sort chronologically, which is valuable for database index locality and simply being able to tell creation order at a glance.</p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Generate a ULID.</li>
    <li>Copy it for use as an identifier in your database or application.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common ULID Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Assuming a ULID hides creation time</strong> — since the timestamp is encoded directly in the ID, anyone can extract roughly when it was created, which may matter for privacy-sensitive contexts.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing ULIDs and UUIDs in the same column</strong> without a clear migration plan, complicating uniform ID handling.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use a ULID</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Using naturally sortable, time-ordered identifiers for database primary keys</li>
    <li>Improving index locality for a table with high insert volume compared to random UUIDs</li>
    <li>Generating IDs where approximate creation order matters without a separate timestamp column</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>ULID FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is a ULID a drop-in replacement for UUID?</strong> Functionally similar as a unique identifier, though ULIDs use a different string format (26 characters, Crockford's Base32) — check compatibility with any systems expecting standard UUID format specifically.</p>
</ToolLayout>
